From 999d803aa287a53fc45e51df99ee39d4685b39f9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 01:28:57 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] score: Run global constructors when built with clang

_Thread_Global_construction() and _exit() decide whether to run global
constructors and destructors with

  #if defined( __USE_INIT_FINI__ )

__USE_INIT_FINI__ is a GCC-internal predefine.  clang does not define it,
and neither does it define __USE__MAIN__, so EXECUTE_GLOBAL_CONSTRUCTORS
is never defined and RTEMS silently runs no C++ static constructor at all.
Nothing references __libc_init_array either, so the PROVIDE_HIDDEN of
__init_array_start and __init_array_end in linkcmds.base never fires and
those symbols are absent from the executable entirely.

The failure mode is not a diagnostic but a crash: the first use of
std::cout faults while loading the virtual base offset from an
unconstructed stream,

  lw a5, 0x0(a1)      # vptr of the ostream
  lw a5, -0xc(a5)     # virtual base offset -> load access fault

clang emits .init_array exactly as gcc does, and every RTEMS port already
selects the mechanism with CPU_USE_LIBC_INIT_FINI_ARRAY, so the guard
simply needs to admit clang.  The gcc path is byte-for-byte unchanged.

On riscv/mbv built with clang this fixes iostream, rcxx01, spglobalcon02
and sptls04.  Confirmed by symbol inspection as well: __init_array_start,
__init_array_end and __libc_init_array are present after the change and
absent before, matching a gcc-built image.

A cleaner long-term fix would be to stop testing a compiler-internal
macro altogether, since RTEMS always uses newlib and every port defines
CPU_USE_LIBC_INIT_FINI_ARRAY, but that is a larger change than this one.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 cpukit/libcsupport/src/newlibc_exit.c | 4 +++-
 cpukit/score/src/threadhandler.c      | 8 +++++++-
 2 files changed, 10 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/cpukit/libcsupport/src/newlibc_exit.c b/cpukit/libcsupport/src/newlibc_exit.c
index 866232478e..e0994b3f21 100644
--- a/cpukit/libcsupport/src/newlibc_exit.c
+++ b/cpukit/libcsupport/src/newlibc_exit.c
@@ -19,7 +19,9 @@
 #include <stdio.h>
 #include <unistd.h>
 
-  #if defined( __USE_INIT_FINI__ )
+  /* See the comment in cpukit/score/src/threadhandler.c: __USE_INIT_FINI__ is
+   * a GCC-internal predefine which clang does not define. */
+  #if defined( __USE_INIT_FINI__ ) || defined( __clang__ )
     #if CPU_USE_LIBC_INIT_FINI_ARRAY == TRUE
       #define FINI_SYMBOL __libc_fini_array
     #else
diff --git a/cpukit/score/src/threadhandler.c b/cpukit/score/src/threadhandler.c
index 5e71172d2d..603be16b96 100644
--- a/cpukit/score/src/threadhandler.c
+++ b/cpukit/score/src/threadhandler.c
@@ -54,7 +54,13 @@ RTEMS_STATIC_ASSERT(
     CPU_USE_LIBC_INIT_FINI_ARRAY == FALSE,
   CPU_USE_LIBC_INIT_FINI_ARRAY
 );
-#if defined( __USE_INIT_FINI__ )
+/*
+ * __USE_INIT_FINI__ is a GCC-internal predefine.  clang does not define it, so
+ * testing for it alone silently disables global construction entirely: no C++
+ * static constructor ever runs.  clang emits .init_array exactly as GCC does,
+ * so treat it the same way.
+ */
+#if defined( __USE_INIT_FINI__ ) || defined( __clang__ )
   #if CPU_USE_LIBC_INIT_FINI_ARRAY == TRUE
     #define INIT_NAME __libc_init_array
   #else
